window.CAPS={"pt": {"01": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.737\nVamos falar de prevalência de\nproblemas de saúde mental em Portugal.\n\n2\n00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.937\nPortugal é um dos países europeus\n\n3\n00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.597\ncom maior\nprevalência de problemas de saúde mental.\n\n4\n00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.757\nEstudos recentes indicam\nque mais de um terço da população\n\n5\n00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.657\nadulta apresenta\nsintomas de ansiedade ou depressão,\n\n6\n00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.177\nsendo que muitos destes casos não recebem\n\n7\n00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.917\nacompanhamento clínico\nou psicológico adequado.\n\n8\n00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.740\nEstes números não representam\napenas estatísticas.\n\n9\n00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.147\nPor detrás de cada percentagem,\nhá pessoas com sofrimento real.\n\n10\n00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.817\nMuitas vezes, esse sofrimento permanece\ninvisível, não porque não exista,\n\n11\n00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.860\nmas porque não é reconhecido, nem pela\nprópria pessoa, nem por quem a rodeia.\n\n12\n00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.837\nNeste contexto, o trabalho assume\num papel particularmente relevante.\n\n13\n00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.800\nPode funcionar como um fator de proteção\nao oferecer estrutura,\n\n14\n00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.180\npropósito e sentido de pertença.\n\n15\n00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.437\nNo entanto, pode também constituir uma\nfonte de pressão, esgotamento e exclusão.\n\n16\n00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.757\nA forma como nos organizamos e vivemos\no trabalho influencia, por isso,\n\n17\n00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.990\nde forma muito decisiva,\na nossa saúde mental.\n", "12": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.567\nSegundo a Organização Mundial da Saúde,\n\n2\n00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.927\na saúde mental corresponde\na um estado de bem-estar\n\n3\n00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.630\nno qual a pessoa reconhece\nas suas capacidades,\n\n4\n00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.687\nconsegue lidar com os desafios da vida,\n\n5\n00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.747\ntrabalhar de forma produtiva\ne contribuir para a sua comunidade.\n\n6\n00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.887\nO bem-estar pode ser descrito\ncomo a perceção do equilíbrio emocional,\n\n7\n00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.667\nsatisfação com a vida\ne sentido de propósito.\n\n8\n00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:27.807\nNo contexto de trabalho, traduz-se\nna experiência de se sentir valorizado,\n\n9\n00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:32.547\nseguro, competente e em equilíbrio\ncom os diferentes domínios da vida.\n\n10\n00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.427\nImporta sublinhar que a saúde mental\ne o bem-estar não são estados estáticos.\n\n11\n00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.507\nVariam ao longo do tempo\ne em função do contexto.\n\n12\n00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.727\nO trabalho,\nenquanto parte central do quotidiano,\n\n13\n00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.047\ntem um impacto direto nessa variação.\n\n14\n00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:50.327\nAssim, pensar em saúde mental\nimplica necessariamente\n\n15\n00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.707\nconsiderar o contexto\nem que a pessoa está inserida,\n\n16\n00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.827\nincluindo as condições de trabalho,\nas relações interpessoais,\n\n17\n00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.127\na organização das tarefas\ne a cultura organizacional.\n\n18\n00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.007\nReconhecer esta interdependência\né um passo essencial\n\n19\n00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.740\npara a criação de ambientes\nmais saudáveis e sustentáveis.\n", "13": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.277\nVamos falar sobre o papel das organizações\nna promoção da saúde mental.\n\n2\n00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.097\nAs organizações desempenham\num papel estratégico\n\n3\n00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.817\nna promoção da saúde mental dos\nseus colaboradores.\n\n4\n00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.437\nNão apenas porque o trabalho ocupa uma\nparte significativa da vida das pessoas,\n\n5\n00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.897\nmas também porque é neste contexto que\nse estruturam muitas das suas relações,\n\n6\n00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.677\nrotinas e exigências.\n\n7\n00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.677\nÉ frequentemente no local de trabalho\n\n8\n00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.637\nque surgem os primeiros\nsinais de sofrimento psicológico,\n\n9\n00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.117\ncomo as alterações no comportamento,\ncansaço extremo, irritabilidade,\n\n10\n00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.617\ndificuldade de concentração ou isolamento.\n\n11\n00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.040\nQuando os contextos organizacionais\nestão atentos e preparados,\n\n12\n00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.057\nestes sinais podem ser\nreconhecidos atempadamente,\n\n13\n00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.777\npermitindo assim\numa resposta mais adequada.\n\n14\n00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.077\nPara além da identificação do mal-estar,\n\n15\n00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.800\no maior potencial das organizações\nreside na prevenção.\n\n16\n00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.577\nPromover ambientes saudáveis implica\no desenvolvimento de uma cultura\n\n17\n00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.457\norganizacional centrada\nno respeito, na escuta,\n\n18\n00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.437\nna justiça e na valorização das pessoas.\n\n19\n00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.177\nQuando estas condições estão presentes,\nas relações tornam-se mais cooperativas,\n\n20\n00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.760\na motivação aumenta,\n\n21\n00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:10.050\na produtividade melhora e o risco de\nburnout e de absentismo diminui.\n\n22\n00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.717\nParalelamente, fatores como o equilíbrio\nentre a vida pessoal e profissional,\n\n23\n00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.837\na segurança no emprego, a confiança na\nliderança e o acesso a apoio psicológico\n\n24\n00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.877\nconstituem elementos fundamentais\nna promoção do bem-estar.\n\n25\n00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.540\nPromover a saúde mental no trabalho\nnão é um elemento acessório,\n\n26\n00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.417\nmas uma dimensão essencial\nno funcionamento organizacional.\n\n27\n00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.337\nTodas as organizações, independentemente\nda sua dimensão ou setor,\n\n28\n00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.450\ntêm um papel ativo neste processo.\n", "21": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.257\nOs riscos psicossociais correspondem\na características do trabalho,\n\n2\n00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.120\ncomo a forma como está organizado,\n\n3\n00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.157\no conteúdo das tarefas\nou a qualidade das relações,\n\n4\n00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.197\nque podem afetar negativamente a saúde\nfísica, emocional e social das pessoas.\n\n5\n00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.740\nEmbora não sejam\nvisíveis de uma forma direta,\n\n6\n00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.760\ncomo um equipamento avariado\nou um espaço inseguro,\n\n7\n00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.737\nos seus efeitos são reais\ne muitas vezes profundos.\n\n8\n00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.337\nA exposição prolongada a estes riscos\n\n9\n00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:32.977\nestá associada a níveis mais elevados de\nstress, a um esgotamento profissional,\n\n10\n00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.560\na ansiedade, depressão e também\na manifestações físicas,\n\n11\n00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.997\ncomo dores musculares\nou alterações do sono.\n\n12\n00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.757\nEntre os exemplos mais comuns,\nencontram-se a sobrecarga de trabalho,\n\n13\n00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.037\na pressão constante por resultados,\na ausência de reconhecimento,\n\n14\n00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.417\na insegurança quanto ao futuro\nou aos conflitos com colegas e chefias.\n\n15\n00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.777\nQuando estas situações persistem\nao longo do tempo, podem contribuir\n\n16\n00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.897\npara a criação de ambientes de trabalho\ntóxicos e prejudiciais.\n\n17\n00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.260\nImporta sublinhar que estes riscos\nnão estão limitados a setores específicos,\n\n18\n00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.937\npodem existir em qualquer área,\nfunção ou organização.\n\n19\n00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:13.717\nPor esse motivo, devem ser reconhecidos,\navaliados e geridos de forma sistemática,\n\n20\n00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.937\ntal como qualquer outro\nrisco profissional.\n\n21\n00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.137\nCriar ambientes de trabalho\npsicologicamente seguros\n\n22\n00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.057\nnão é apenas uma obrigação ética,\n\n23\n00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.597\né também uma estratégia eficaz\npara promover o bem-estar\n\n24\n00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.937\ne melhorar o desempenho organizacional.\n\n25\n00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.297\nVamos então falar dos tipos de\nrisco psicossociais no trabalho.\n\n26\n00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.957\nPrimeiro, as exigências laborais.\n\n27\n00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.377\nAs exigências laborais\nreferem-se ao volume\n\n28\n00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.897\ne à intensidade das tarefas\nque nos são atribuídas.\n\n29\n00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.277\nNesta dimensão encontram-se\nas exigências quantitativas,\n\n30\n00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.777\nque dizem respeito\nà quantidade de trabalho,\n\n31\n00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.497\nprazos apertados e carga excessiva.\n\n32\n00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.497\nO ritmo do trabalho elevado\n\n33\n00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.157\ne constante pode gerar\numa sensação de urgência permanente.\n\n34\n00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.577\nAs exigências cognitivas implicam manter\na atenção, tomar decisões rápidas,\n\n35\n00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.980\nlidar com informações complexas\ne as exigências emocionais envolvem,\n\n36\n00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.477\npor exemplo, ter de lidar\ncom o sofrimento alheio,\n\n37\n00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.737\nesconder as emoções ou gerir\nconflitos frequentes.\n\n38\n00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.397\nQuando estas exigências são contínuas\ne não acompanhadas de recursos ou apoio,\n\n39\n00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.537\npodem levar a um esgotamento\nfísico e emocional.\n\n40\n00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.417\nEm segundo, o segundo risco é\na organização do trabalho e conteúdo.\n\n41\n00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.077\nAqui olhamos para a forma como o trabalho\nestá estruturado e o conteúdo das tarefas.\n\n42\n00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.357\nNesta dimensão encontram-se\na influência no trabalho,\n\n43\n00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.400\nque diz respeito ao grau de autonomia,\n\n44\n00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.837\npodemos tomar decisões\nou apenas executar ordens,\n\n45\n00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.660\na possibilidade de desenvolvimento,\n\n46\n00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.457\nque se refere à existência de\noportunidades de aprendizagem\n\n47\n00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.377\ne crescimento, o significado do trabalho,\n\n48\n00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.537\nque está relacionado com a perceção de\nque aquilo que fazemos tem valor,\n\n49\n00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.777\no compromisso face ao local de trabalho,\nque se traduz no envolvimento,\n\n50\n00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.040\nna identificação com os objetivos da\norganização e no sentimento de pertença,\n\n51\n00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.237\ne ambientes que não permitem crescimento\n\n52\n00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.940\nnem reconhecem\no valor do trabalho realizado,\n\n53\n00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.217\ntendem a gerar frustração e desmotivação.\n\n54\n00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.037\nO terceiro tipo de risco são\nrelações sociais e de liderança.\n\n55\n00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.297\nEste é um dos domínios mais relevantes\npara a saúde mental no trabalho.\n\n56\n00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.817\nNesta dimensão encontram-se\na previsibilidade,\n\n57\n00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.757\nque está relacionada com a\nclareza das funções e das expectativas.\n\n58\n00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.800\nA recompensa pode ser financeira,\n\n59\n00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.057\nmas também inclui\num reconhecimento e valorização.\n\n60\n00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.160\nA transparência é, por isso,\nessencial para a confiança:\n\n61\n00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.880\nsaber o que se espera de nós,\ncomo somos avaliados,\n\n62\n00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.637\nque decisões estão a ser tomadas.\n\n63\n00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.537\nO conflito de papéis surge\nquando há exigências contraditórias\n\n64\n00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.837\nou a falta de definição de\nresponsabilidades.\n\n65\n00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.217\nA qualidade da liderança é determinante.\n\n66\n00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.017\nLideranças autoritárias, ausentes\nou incoerentes geram tensão e insegurança.\n\n67\n00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.357\nO apoio social de colegas e superiores\natua como fator de proteção.\n\n68\n00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.560\nQuando há empatia,\ndisponibilidade e colaboração,\n\n69\n00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.017\no impacto do stress tende a ser menor.\n\n70\n00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:59.237\nRelações pobres ou tóxicas\nno trabalho são uma das principais\n\n71\n00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.497\ncausas de mal-estar emocional\ne de abandono da função.\n\n72\n00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.837\nO quarto tipo de risco é\na interface trabalho-indivíduo.\n\n73\n00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.237\nEste tipo de risco\nrelaciona-se com a forma\n\n74\n00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.117\ncomo o trabalho se articula\ncom outras dimensões da vida.\n\n75\n00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.040\nNesta dimensão,\nencontram-se a insegurança laboral,\n\n76\n00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:19.197\ncomo o receio de perder\no emprego ou contratos instáveis,\n\n77\n00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.297\nque gera ansiedade prolongada,\na satisfação com o trabalho,\n\n78\n00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.937\nque é um indicador importante de\nbem-estar: se estamos insatisfeitos,\n\n79\n00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.137\né mais provável que surjam\nsintomas de burnout ou desmotivação.\n\n80\n00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.137\nO conflito trabalho-família surge\n\n81\n00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.657\nquando as exigências profissionais\ninterferem com a vida pessoal,\n\n82\n00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.020\no que faz com que haja falta de tempo\npara estar com os filhos,\n\n83\n00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.797\ndescansar ou cuidar da saúde.\n\n84\n00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.337\nSão alguns dos exemplos mais concretos.\n\n85\n00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.077\nAmbientes que não respeitam\neste equilíbrio comprometem\n\n86\n00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.077\na saúde dos trabalhadores\ne a sua produtividade a médio prazo.\n\n87\n00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.257\nO quinto tipo de risco psicossocial\nsão os valores no local de trabalho.\n\n88\n00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.797\nA cultura organizacional\ninfluencia fortemente a forma\n\n89\n00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.117\ncomo as pessoas se sentem\nno seu dia a dia.\n\n90\n00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.560\nNesta dimensão encontram-se\na confiança vertical, ou seja,\n\n91\n00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.080\nentre quem lidera e quem é liderado,\n\n92\n00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.257\ne esta confiança é essencial\npara uma boa comunicação\n\n93\n00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.237\ne resolução de conflitos e de problemas.\n\n94\n00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:18.280\nA confiança horizontal,\nisto é, entre colegas,\n\n95\n00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.320\nfacilita a colaboração\ne o espírito de equipa.\n\n96\n00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:25.260\nA justiça e o respeito envolvem\no tratamento equitativo,\n\n97\n00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.177\no reconhecimento das diferenças\ne a escuta ativa.\n\n98\n00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.837\nA comunidade social no trabalho refere-se\nà qualidade das relações informais,\n\n99\n00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.557\nao sentimento de pertença, à ideia de que\nfazemos algo ou fazemos parte de algo.\n\n100\n00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.057\nQuando os valores estão\ndesalinhados com esta prática\n\n101\n00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.217\nou quando se verifica\nhipocrisia institucional,\n\n102\n00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.857\nos efeitos são corrosivos para a motivação\ne para a saúde mental dos colaboradores.\n\n103\n00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.857\nO sexto tipo de risco são\nos comportamentos ofensivos.\n\n104\n00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.377\nEste é um dos riscos mais graves\ne mais difíceis de reportar.\n\n105\n00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.520\nNesta dimensão encontram-se o assédio\nmoral, onde existe humilhação repetida,\n\n106\n00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.417\ndesvalorização,\nsabotagem ou isolamento social.\n\n107\n00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.757\nO assédio sexual, com comentários,\n\n108\n00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.057\ngestos ou comportamentos\nindesejados de natureza sexual.\n\n109\n00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:17.757\nA discriminação, com base em género,\netnia, orientação sexual, idade,\n\n110\n00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.937\ndeficiência, entre outros.\n\n111\n00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:24.777\nA violência verbal ou física,\nainda que subtil ou disfarçada.\n\n112\n00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.857\nE estes comportamentos que falámos\nviolam os direitos humanos básicos\n\n113\n00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.237\ne têm um impacto devastador\nna saúde mental e na autoestima.\n\n114\n00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.417\nOs riscos psicossociais\nnão são inevitáveis,\n\n115\n00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:39.137\nmas surgem quando o trabalho\nnão é bem planeado, gerido,\n\n116\n00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:42.777\nou quando o contexto social\nnão protege o trabalhador.\n\n117\n00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.237\nIdentificar é o primeiro passo\npara prevenir o sofrimento psicológico\n\n118\n00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.150\ne promover ambientes\nlaborais mais saudáveis.\n", "24": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.597\nVamos falar sobre fatores individuais\nque influenciam a perceção dos riscos.\n\n2\n00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.017\nCada pessoa é única e isso reflete-se\nna forma como vive o trabalho.\n\n3\n00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.200\nDuas pessoas podem estar na mesma\nequipa, com as mesmas tarefas,\n\n4\n00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.657\no mesmo horário e as mesmas lideranças\n\n5\n00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.057\ne ainda assim ter experiências\ncompletamente diferentes.\n\n6\n00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.657\nEnquanto uma se sente\nmotivada e tranquila,\n\n7\n00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.997\na outra pode sentir-se esgotada,\nansiosa ou à beira de desistir.\n\n8\n00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.237\nO que explica esta diferença?\n\n9\n00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.520\nA resposta está nos fatores\nindividuais, ou seja,\n\n10\n00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.237\nem tudo aquilo que cada pessoa traz\nconsigo para o contexto de trabalho.\n\n11\n00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.497\nEstes fatores influenciam a forma\ncomo interpretamos as situações,\n\n12\n00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.737\nreagimos ao stress e utilizamos,\nou não, estratégias de regulação.\n\n13\n00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.117\nDe seguida, apresentam-se\nalguns dos principais.\n\n14\n00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.857\nExperiências de vida adversas.\n\n15\n00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.000\nPessoas que passaram por situações\ndifíceis ao longo da vida,\n\n16\n00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.797\ncomo negligência, violência,\n\n17\n00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.397\nluto ou pobreza podem apresentar\numa maior sensibilidade ao stress.\n\n18\n00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.797\nDeterminados contextos\nno trabalho podem ativar respostas\n\n19\n00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.300\nemocionais associadas\na experiências anteriores,\n\n20\n00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.060\nmesmo que o risco atual\nnão seja equivalente.\n\n21\n00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.797\nPor exemplo, uma pessoa\n\n22\n00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.637\nque viveu insegurança habitacional\nna infância pode reagir\n\n23\n00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.717\ncom elevada ansiedade\na um anúncio de reestruturação da empresa,\n\n24\n00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.060\nmesmo antes de saber\nse será diretamente afetada.\n\n25\n00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.657\nHistória familiar e fatores genéticos.\n\n26\n00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.417\nA presença de perturbações mentais na\nfamília, como a depressão ou a ansiedade,\n\n27\n00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.377\npodem aumentar\na vulnerabilidade individual,\n\n28\n00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.377\nsobretudo quando não existem\nestratégias de coping\n\n29\n00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.017\ndesenvolvidas ou suporte adequado.\n\n30\n00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.317\nPor exemplo, uma colaboradora\ncuja mãe teve depressão grave\n\n31\n00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.597\npode interpretar críticas\nno trabalho de forma mais pessoal,\n\n32\n00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.557\nexperienciando-as como rejeição.\n\n33\n00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.500\nFatores biológicos. Aspetos\nbiológicos como alterações hormonais,\n\n34\n00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.917\nperturbações do sono, doenças crónicas\nou dor persistente influenciam diretamente\n\n35\n00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.877\na capacidade de regulação\nemocional e a tolerância ao stress.\n\n36\n00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.140\nA resposta ao trabalho não é apenas\npsicológica, é também corporal.\n\n37\n00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.837\nTemperamento\ne características psicológicas.\n\n38\n00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.020\nAs diferenças individuais\nao nível do temperamento,\n\n39\n00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.237\ncomo maior tendência para ansiedade,\nimpulsividade ou perfeccionismo,\n\n40\n00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.137\nmoldam a forma como cada\npessoa responde a exigências,\n\n41\n00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.520\nprazos ou situações de incerteza.\n\n42\n00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.237\nPor exemplo, uma pessoa\n\n43\n00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.877\ncom traços de perfeccionismo\npode sentir elevado desconforto\n\n44\n00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:31.557\nem contextos com ambiguidade ou onde os\nerros são mais frequentes e inevitáveis.\n\n45\n00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.680\nCondições socioeconómicas.\n\n46\n00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.620\nA instabilidade financeira,\n\n47\n00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.457\na precariedade habitacional\nou a responsabilidade por dependentes\n\n48\n00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.177\naumentam a pressão sobre o indivíduo\ne reduzem a margem de adaptação.\n\n49\n00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.837\nEstas condições tendem a amplificar\no impacto dos riscos psicossociais.\n\n50\n00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.757\nPor exemplo, um trabalhador\n\n51\n00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.957\nque depende exclusivamente do seu salário\npara sustentar\n\n52\n00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.877\na família pode tolerar condições\nadversas por receio de perder o emprego.\n\n53\n00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.437\nConsumo de substâncias.\n\n54\n00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.957\nO consumo de álcool,\n\n55\n00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.020\nmedicação ou outras substâncias\npode constituir um fator de risco,\n\n56\n00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.077\nmas também uma tentativa de lidar\ncom o stress ou a sobrecarga.\n\n57\n00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.837\nNo entanto, quando utilizado\ncomo estratégia principal,\n\n58\n00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.337\ntende a agravar o problema,\ncriando ciclos de maior vulnerabilidade.\n\n59\n00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.897\nApoio familiar e social.\n\n60\n00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.057\nA existência de uma rede de apoio sólida,\nfamiliar, social ou profissional,\n\n61\n00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.877\nfunciona como um importante\nfator de proteção.\n\n62\n00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.277\nPessoas que se sentem apoiadas tendem\n\n63\n00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.137\na recuperar mais rapidamente\nsituações difíceis.\n\n64\n00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.017\nPor exemplo, após um conflito no trabalho,\n\n65\n00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.077\na possibilidade de partilhar a experiência\n\n66\n00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.017\ncom alguém de confiança pode reduzir\nsignificativamente o impacto emocional.\n\n67\n00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.377\nA saúde física. A presença de\nproblemas de saúde, como a fadiga crónica,\n\n68\n00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.397\ndor persistente\nou doenças de longa duração,\n\n69\n00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.660\npode comprometer a capacidade de lidar\ncom exigências profissionais,\n\n70\n00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.077\ntanto físicas como emocionais.\n\n71\n00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.237\nOs riscos psicossociais\nnão atuam de forma isolada.\n\n72\n00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.077\nO seu impacto é profundamente influenciado\n\n73\n00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.697\npelas características\nindividuais de cada pessoa.\n\n74\n00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.637\nAssim, promover ambientes de trabalho\nsaudáveis não implica\n\n75\n00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.617\napenas reduzir fatores de risco externos,\n\n76\n00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.717\nmas também reconhecer\na diversidade de experiências,\n\n77\n00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.650\ntrajetos e necessidades\nindividuais presentes nas equipas.\n", "36": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.677\nVamos falar de fatores de risco\ne fatores protetores.\n\n2\n00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.077\nQuando alguém passa\npor uma situação de stress intenso\n\n3\n00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.497\nou potencialmente traumática, a forma como\nreage não depende apenas do evento em si.\n\n4\n00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.277\nDepende também de um conjunto de fatores\nque existem antes,\n\n5\n00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.357\ndurante e depois da situação.\n\n6\n00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.817\nE isto pode aumentar o risco de sofrimento\nduradouro ou, pelo contrário,\n\n7\n00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.837\nfacilitar uma recuperação saudável.\n\n8\n00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.917\nAntes da crise,\nhá uma vulnerabilidade e proteção prévia.\n\n9\n00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.677\nAntes mesmo de o evento acontecer,\n\n10\n00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.017\nalgumas pessoas já apresentam\nfatores de vulnerabilidade.\n\n11\n00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.677\nEntre estes, incluem-se\num historial de trauma\n\n12\n00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.077\nou experiências adversas ao longo da vida,\n\n13\n00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.277\nbem como uma saúde mental\npreviamente fragilizada.\n\n14\n00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.597\nEstes elementos podem aumentar\na sensibilidade ao stress\n\n15\n00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.277\ne dificultar a regulação\nemocional em situações exigentes.\n\n16\n00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.637\nPor outro lado,\n\n17\n00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.257\nexistem também fatores protetores prévios\nque podem atenuar o impacto de uma crise.\n\n18\n00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.757\nEntre estes destacam-se a existência de\nvínculos sociais positivos,\n\n19\n00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.977\nseja com familiares, colegas ou comunidade\nno geral, bem como a literacia emocional\n\n20\n00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.917\ne a utilização de estratégias de adaptação\neficazes.\n\n21\n00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.877\nExperiências anteriores de superação\n\n22\n00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.757\ne contextos organizacionais\nmarcados por uma cultura de cuidado,\n\n23\n00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.517\nprevenção e escuta constituem\nigualmente elementos protetores.\n\n24\n00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.897\nEstes fatores não eliminam\no impacto da crise,\n\n25\n00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.277\nmas influenciam significativamente\na forma como esta é vivida.\n\n26\n00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.917\nDurante a crise,\nhá uma resposta imediata do contexto.\n\n27\n00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.577\nDurante um momento crítico,\ncomo um acidente,\n\n28\n00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.637\num conflito intenso ou um despedimento\nou outro evento desregulador,\n\n29\n00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.417\nos fatores presentes no contexto imediato\npodem agravar ou atenuar o sofrimento.\n\n30\n00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.117\nA ausência de uma comunicação\nclara e empática,\n\n31\n00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.877\nlideranças desorganizadas ou ausentes,\n\n32\n00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.217\no isolamento emocional da pessoa\nafetada ou respostas punitivas\n\n33\n00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.977\ne desvalorizadoras\npor parte da organização\n\n34\n00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.717\nconstituem fatores de risco importantes.\n\n35\n00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.617\nEm contraste, a presença de apoio\nimediato pode funcionar\n\n36\n00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.777\ncomo um fator protetor significativo.\n\n37\n00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.800\nEstar acompanhado por alguém\nque escuta com empatia,\n\n38\n00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.257\no reconhecimento do impacto da situação,\n\n39\n00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.217\na existência de um espaço físico\ne emocional seguro para reagir\n\n40\n00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.917\ne a intervenção\nrápida de alguém com formação\n\n41\n00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.417\nem primeiros socorros psicológicos\nsão elementos\n\n42\n00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.417\nque contribuem\npara a estabilização inicial.\n\n43\n00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.720\nNo contexto laboral,\n\n44\n00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.977\na forma como a organização responde\nneste momento pode ser determinante.\n\n45\n00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.357\nApós a crise, em que há\numa recuperação e acompanhamento,\n\n46\n00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.157\nas semanas e meses seguintes\nà situação são igualmente relevantes\n\n47\n00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.817\npara o processo de recuperação.\n\n48\n00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.797\nUma retoma rápida das atividades\nsem tempo adequado de recuperação,\n\n49\n00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.357\no silêncio institucional\nou a negação do impacto da situação,\n\n50\n00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.957\na ausência de acompanhamento psicológico\n\n51\n00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.157\ne a manutenção de contextos de trabalho\nadversos, como pressão excessiva,\n\n52\n00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.257\nconflitos ou isolamento,\npodem agravar o sofrimento.\n\n53\n00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.257\nPor outro lado, a existência de seguimento\npor parte da chefia ou dos pares,\n\n54\n00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:06.180\no acesso a apoio psicológico, a realização\nde ações de cuidado e solidariedade e,\n\n55\n00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.057\nquando necessário,\na adaptação das condições de trabalho,\n\n56\n00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.277\nfuncionam como fatores\nprotetores importantes nesta fase.\n\n57\n00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.737\nEm síntese, o impacto de uma crise\nnão depende apenas do evento em si,\n\n58\n00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:21.140\nmas do conjunto de condições individuais\ne organizacionais que a antecedem,\n\n59\n00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.597\nque a acompanham e que a sucedem.\n\n60\n00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.677\nIntervir sobre estes fatores permite\n\n61\n00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.357\numa abordagem\nmais estruturada da saúde mental,\n\n62\n00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.797\ncentrada não apenas na resposta à crise,\n\n63\n00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.750\nmas também na sua prevenção\ne gestão ao longo do tempo.\n", "4a": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.337\nUma das estratégias de autorregulação que\npodes utilizar é a respiração controlada.\n\n2\n00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.537\nE para isso vou-te ensinar alguns truques\n\n3\n00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.490\npara que ela possa ser\nfeita de forma eficaz.\n\n4\n00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.537\nPrimeiramente, vou-te pedir\nque te sentes num sítio confortável,\n\n5\n00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.502\ncom os pés bem apoiados no chão.\n\n6\n00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.197\nDepois, dizer-te que o objetivo é\ninspirar pelo nariz e expirar pela boca.\n\n7\n00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.377\nSe ajudar, podes pensar em cheirar\numa flor, soprar uma vela.\n\n8\n00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.597\nPosteriormente, vou-te pedir que coloques\numa das tuas mãos por cima do peito\n\n9\n00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.480\ne outra das tuas mãos por baixo do peito,\n\n10\n00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.697\npara que consigas sentir\nmelhor a tua respiração.\n\n11\n00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.237\nE agora o objetivo será inspirar,\nsuster e expirar, ok?\n\n12\n00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.150\nVou fazer contigo\npara que seja mais fácil.\n\n13\n00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.270\nInspirar, suster,\n\n14\n00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.270\nexpirar.\n\n15\n00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.527\nVamos fazer isto pensando em segundos.\n\n16\n00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.867\nCinco segundos para inspirar,\ntrês segundos para suster,\n\n17\n00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.860\ncinco segundos para expirar.\n\n18\n00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:16.880\nFaz comigo.\n\n19\n00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.287\nEsta respiração é suposto\nque sintas o teu peito, ou seja,\n\n20\n00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.707\nas tuas duas mãos a subir ao mesmo tempo.\n\n21\n00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:40.307\nO ar por cima do peito e por baixo do\npeito tem que entrar ao mesmo tempo.\n\n22\n00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.087\nVamos fazer novamente.\n\n23\n00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.360\nInspirar em cinco,\nsuster três e expirar em cinco.\n\n24\n00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.870\nO objetivo da respiração é\nrenovar o ar dos teus pulmões,\n\n25\n00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.907\nabrandar o coração e fazer-te\nfocar numa tarefa simples\n\n26\n00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:11.680\nque te vai fazer pensar e vai-te\ntrazer para o momento presente.\n", "4b": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.507\nO grounding é\numa estratégia de autorregulação que serve\n\n2\n00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.247\npara nos trazer para o momento presente\ne para nos ajudar a sair, muitas vezes,\n\n3\n00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.767\nde um ciclo vicioso de pensamentos.\n\n4\n00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.647\nA verdade é que nós não temos\na capacidade de calar a mente,\n\n5\n00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.970\nmas temos a capacidade de direcioná-la.\n\n6\n00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:28.347\nE por isso, dar uma tarefa simples\nao nosso cérebro ajuda-nos a autorregular.\n\n7\n00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.667\nPara isso, vamos usar os nossos cinco\nsentidos e por ordem decrescente.\n\n8\n00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:38.507\nO objetivo é que me consigas dizer,\nno ambiente envolvente,\n\n9\n00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.300\ncinco coisas que consegues ver.\n\n10\n00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:46.107\nQuatro coisas que consigas tocar.\n\n11\n00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.007\nQuando reparas nessas\ncoisas que podes tocar,\n\n12\n00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:55.747\no que eu te sugiro é que toques e que\nprocures sentir as texturas diferentes.\n\n13\n00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.340\nOu seja, quatro coisas que podes tocar.\n\n14\n00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.180\nTrês coisas que podes ouvir.\n\n15\n00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.627\nDuas coisas que podes cheirar.\n\n16\n00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.587\nE, por fim, uma coisa que podes saborear.\n\n17\n00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.807\nNeste momento, se tiveres efetivamente\nalguma coisa que possas saborear,\n\n18\n00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:20.630\né importante que te demores a saborear\nisso, seja um rebuçado, uma peça de fruta,\n\n19\n00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.320\nseja um chocolate, o que quer que seja.\n\n20\n00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.287\nRelativamente às coisas\nque nós às vezes podemos pensar\n\n21\n00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.570\nou perguntar e não conseguir\nencontrar a resposta, por exemplo,\n\n22\n00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.787\nduas coisas que eu consigo cheirar.\n\n23\n00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.867\nÀs vezes não conseguimos\ncheirar duas coisas\n\n24\n00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.527\nou não conseguimos sentir dois odores.\n\n25\n00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.247\nO que eu te convido a fazer\nnesse momento é: ou ignoras,\n\n26\n00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:46.687\nse sentires que estás muito ativado ainda,\nou então recorrer à memória,\n\n27\n00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.547\nporque o nosso cérebro tem uma capacidade\nde memória e eu posso pensar, ok,\n\n28\n00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.307\nnão consigo cheirar duas coisas,\n\n29\n00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.807\nmas consigo recordar\num cheiro que eu gosto muito.\n\n30\n00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.667\nPor exemplo,\ngosto muito do cheiro das ondas do mar\n\n31\n00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.267\ne o meu cérebro automaticamente\n\n32\n00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.047\nvai relembrar essa sensação\ne ajudar-me a permitir\n\n33\n00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.920\nque essa sensação percorra o meu\ncorpo e ajudar-me a autorregular, ok?\n\n34\n00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:18.470\nPor isso, recapitulando, cinco coisas que\npodes ver, quatro coisas que podes tocar,\n\n35\n00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.387\ntrês coisas que podes ouvir,\n\n36\n00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:27.020\nduas coisas que podes cheirar\ne uma coisa que podes provar.\n", "4c": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.317\nA estratégia STOP é uma estratégia\nque pode ser utilizada em qualquer lugar,\n\n2\n00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.510\npois é uma estratégia que podes\nfazer baixinho ou até mentalmente.\n\n3\n00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.117\nSTOP porque é «stop» de «para».\n\n4\n00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.790\nPara o que estás a fazer.\n\n5\n00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.600\nRespira.\n\n6\n00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.437\nRespira lentamente, uma ou duas vezes,\ndurante um ou dois minutos,\n\n7\n00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.049\na respiração controlada de que também\nfalamos neste toolkit.\n\n8\n00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.977\nObserva, ou seja, observa o que está à tua\nvolta, observa o que está a acontecer,\n\n9\n00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.457\nobserva as sensações no teu corpo,\nas emoções, o teu comportamento,\n\n10\n00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.277\no teu pensamento.\n\n11\n00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.497\nDepois,\n\n12\n00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.090\nmuda a perspetiva.\n\n13\n00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.457\nOk, o que está a acontecer?\n\n14\n00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.037\nHá outra forma de lidar com isto?\n\n15\n00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.700\nHá outra explicação para isto?\n\n16\n00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.077\nEu posso mudar esta\nperspetiva de pensamento?\n\n17\n00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.980\nE, por fim, procede, ou seja,\n\n18\n00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:10.677\ncomeça por comportamentos ou ações\npequenas, mas cria movimento,\n\n19\n00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.077\nou seja, procede.\n\n20\n00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.977\nEsta estratégia é extremamente importante\ne permite-nos parar para nos ouvirmos,\n\n21\n00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.090\nque é algo que é extremamente\nimportante na autorregulação.\n", "4d": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.337\nA autorregulação através do toque é\numa estratégia que pode ser utilizada\n\n2\n00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.297\nquando estamos sob\nmuito stress ou ansiedade\n\n3\n00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:20.190\ne consiste em buscarmos regulação\natravés do toque em nós próprios.\n\n4\n00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.857\nExistem várias e eu vou-vos\nensinar quatro formas de o fazer.\n\n5\n00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.680\nA primeira que vos quero ensinar tem\na ver com o colocar a mão no peito,\n\n6\n00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.077\numa mão acima do peito\ne outra abaixo do peito.\n\n7\n00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.960\nÉ uma estratégia também utilizada\nna respiração controlada,\n\n8\n00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.997\nque está presente aqui\nneste toolkit também.\n\n9\n00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.340\nO objetivo é que vocês sintam,\nmas se juntarem à respiração,\n\n10\n00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.737\njuntam também a sensação de sentir o peito\n\n11\n00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.130\ne a barriga a subir e a descer.\n\n12\n00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.177\nOutra estratégia que podem utilizar tem\na ver com o toque nas próprias mãos.\n\n13\n00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:00.420\nSe nós colocarmos as mãos\nem contacto connosco próprios,\n\n14\n00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.637\npodemos fazer uma ligeira pressão,\n\n15\n00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.917\nquase como se nós estivéssemos a confortar\nalguém, como é que nós faríamos?\n\n16\n00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.017\nEssa pressão deve ser a pressão\nque deves utilizar em ti.\n\n17\n00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.677\nOutra estratégia que podes utilizar\n\n18\n00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.197\né aquela de os braços\nà volta do nosso corpo,\n\n19\n00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.157\nquase como se estivéssemos\na dar um abraço a nós próprios,\n\n20\n00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.677\nquase como se nos estivéssemos\na confortar.\n\n21\n00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:28.177\nEste autoabraço deve também ter alguma\npressão, suave, não deve ser sufocante,\n\n22\n00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.937\ndeve ser tranquila e de conforto, ok?\n\n23\n00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:38.277\nE por fim, o colocar os pés em contacto\ncom o chão e fazer uma ligeira pressão,\n\n24\n00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.457\npara percebermos que estamos aqui,\nno momento presente,\n\n25\n00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.617\nquase como um pensamento de enraizamento,\nok?\n\n26\n00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.417\nEstas estratégias podem ser\nutilizadas e feitas durante\n\n27\n00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:56.070\n30 segundos a um minuto e o objetivo\né quase como um autoconforto.\n", "4e": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.437\n– Maria, posso falar um bocadinho contigo?\n– Sim, diz.\n\n2\n00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.257\nTenho notado que tens estado mais calada,\nmais quieta um bocadinho desde o acidente.\n\n3\n00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.752\nQueria saber como estás.\n\n4\n00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.550\nNão tem sido fácil.\n\n5\n00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.557\nQueres partilhar o que tens\nsentido desde o acidente?\n\n6\n00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.570\nAquilo está-me sempre na cabeça,\nnão consigo deixar de pensar.\n\n7\n00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.157\nE depois já cometi erros\nque não são normais em mim.\n\n8\n00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.437\nImagino, não deve ter sido\nnada fácil assistires a isso.\n\n9\n00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.377\nMas a verdade é que a reação\nque estás a ter é bastante comum\n\n10\n00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.837\nquando estamos presentes\nnum momento desses.\n\n11\n00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.977\nNão há nada de errado contigo.\n\n12\n00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.470\nPois, mas está a afetar o meu trabalho.\n\n13\n00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.240\nO que é que achas\nque te está a custar mais?\n\n14\n00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.777\nÉ estar aqui, no mesmo ambiente de\ntrabalho, ou é a carga de trabalho?\n\n15\n00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.417\nEu acho que é um bocado os dois.\n\n16\n00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.097\nNão estou a conseguir focar-me.\n\n17\n00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.660\nNão é bem falta de foco.\n\n18\n00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.112\nÉ uma reação natural àquilo\nque experienciaste, que viste.\n\n19\n00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.497\nO que é que achas que podemos fazer\npara te trazer mais leveza neste momento?\n\n20\n00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.777\nSe fizer sentido para ti, a MC tem várias\nformas de te prestar apoio psicológico.\n\n21\n00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.517\nE, se quiseres,\neu posso ajudar-te a fazer este contacto.\n\n22\n00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.597\nNão precisas de passar\npor isto sozinha, ok?\n\n23\n00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.937\nObrigada, a sério.\n\n24\n00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.317\nNos próximos dias vamos\nfazendo um follow-up\n\n25\n00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.517\npara perceber se as estratégias\nestão a funcionar e,\n\n26\n00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.377\nse vês que precisas antes disso,\né só dizer-me, está bem?\n\n27\n00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.460\nOk.\n", "4f": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.550\nEstás bem?\n\n2\n00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.050\nSim, não é nada demais.\n\n3\n00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.857\nHum, não pareces muito tu hoje.\n\n4\n00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.870\nSe tiveres de falar, estou aqui.\n\n5\n00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.137\nÉ que hoje de manhã ligaram-me\n\n6\n00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.030\ne a minha mãe foi\ndiagnosticada com aquela doença.\n\n7\n00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.976\nAnda aqui, vamos sentar um bocadinho.\n\n8\n00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.297\nLamento imenso que te sintas assim,\n\n9\n00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.157\ndeve ter sido muito difícil\nreceber essa notícia.\n\n10\n00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.037\nSim, não consigo parar de pensar nisto e,\nao mesmo tempo, estar aqui a trabalhar.\n\n11\n00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.880\nMas faz sentido que não consigas,\nquer dizer,\n\n12\n00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.197\ncontinuar com o teu dia a dia normalmente\n\n13\n00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.917\ndepois de teres recebido\numa notícia dessas.\n\n14\n00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.824\nEstás a processar muita coisa\nao mesmo tempo.\n\n15\n00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.757\nSinto o peito apertado e, ao mesmo tempo,\nestou sem energia para nada,\n\n16\n00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.134\nsó queria sair daqui.\n\n17\n00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.597\nCalma, vamos respirar um bocadinho.\n\n18\n00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.977\nRespira fundo. O que é que tu achas\n\n19\n00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.537\nque te ajudava mais agora?\n\n20\n00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.197\nFicar aqui mais um bocado?\n\n21\n00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.877\nFalar com alguém?\n\n22\n00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.470\nIr para casa mais cedo?\n\n23\n00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.277\nEu acho que hoje estou\na precisar mesmo de sair.\n\n24\n00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.477\nSair mais cedo.\n\n25\n00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.070\nFaz sentido. Queres que vá\ncontigo falar com a nossa chefe?\n\n26\n00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.577\nE não precisas de passar\npor isso sozinha, ok?\n\n27\n00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.977\nObrigada. Estava mesmo\na sentir-me perdida.\n\n28\n00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.297\nOlha, e se continuar a ser muito pesado\npara ti, se te fizer sentido,\n\n29\n00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.177\nnão precisas de estar\na passar por isto sozinha.\n\n30\n00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.037\nPodes falar com alguém.\n\n31\n00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.017\nEu sei que a MC tem, por exemplo, acordos,\n\n32\n00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.117\npodes falar com profissionais de\nsaúde mental, por exemplo.\n\n33\n00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.317\nMas agora vamos com calma,\npasso a passo, ok?\n\n34\n00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.877\nVamos falar com a nossa chefe juntas?\n\n35\n00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.313\nSim. E obrigada por teres falado comigo.\n", "4g": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.087\nPedi-te há bocado para trazeres\nmais bananas e ainda não estão aqui.\n\n2\n00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.287\nTemos o expositor vazio.\n\n3\n00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.387\nEu sei, mas olha, pediram-me para tirar\na fruta que não estava em condições\n\n4\n00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.467\ne depois pediram-me\najuda nos legumes.\n\n5\n00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.947\nSim, mas as bananas são\no produto que sai mais.\n\n6\n00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.547\n– Dá mau aspeto à loja.\n– Eu sei, mas eu não chego a todo o lado.\n\n7\n00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.347\nPois, mas parece que nunca consegues\nacabar nada do que eu te digo.\n\n8\n00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.312\nOlha, e tu parece que só\ntu é que trabalhas aqui.\n\n9\n00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.750\nAna, Maria, podem vir aqui?\n\n10\n00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.957\nEu sei que hoje estão aqui muitos clientes\n\n11\n00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.017\ne que vocês estão um bocadinho\nstressadas com esta situação,\n\n12\n00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.157\nmas nós precisamos de resolver esta\nsituação o mais rápido possível.\n\n13\n00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.737\nO que é que está a acontecer?\n\n14\n00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.637\nAs bananas estão sempre a acabar\n\n15\n00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.440\ne eu estou aqui sozinha a tentar\nmanter tudo apresentável.\n\n16\n00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.217\nEu pedi ajuda e não obtive resposta.\n\n17\n00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.437\nOu seja,\nestás preocupada aqui com a imagem\n\n18\n00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.097\ne com a disposição dos teus produtos?\n\n19\n00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.830\n– Sim.\n– Maria?\n\n20\n00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.037\nEu estive a retirar a fruta\nque não estava apresentável para o cliente\n\n21\n00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.260\ne depois pediram-me\najuda na outra reposição.\n\n22\n00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.737\nNão consegui chegar a todo o lado.\n\n23\n00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.137\nOk, então estás-me a dizer que estás\num bocadinho preocupada aqui\n\n24\n00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:35.357\ncom a manutenção dos produtos\ne com a qualidade?\n\n25\n00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.197\nSim.\n\n26\n00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.397\nEntão, aquilo que eu estou a perceber,\n\n27\n00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.040\nvocês as duas estão a tentar dar resposta\nàs necessidades da secção, ou seja,\n\n28\n00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.257\nnão é uma falta de vontade de ajudar\npor parte de ambas.\n\n29\n00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.637\nAquilo que me parece que está\na acontecer é uma falta de comunicação\n\n30\n00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.990\ne assim fica muito mais difícil\nconseguirmos organizar as prioridades.\n\n31\n00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.657\nEntão, Maria, se tu fores desviada para\noutra tarefa, o que é que podes fazer?\n\n32\n00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.517\nPosso avisar a Ana ou quem\nesteja a trabalhar comigo,\n\n33\n00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.577\npara que saibam com o que podem contar.\n\n34\n00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.817\nBoa, assim eu consigo antecipar.\n\n35\n00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.457\nBom, muito bom esforço por parte de ambas.\n\n36\n00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.017\nEntão, Ana, e agora, se algum\nproduto não estiver em conformidade,\n\n37\n00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.017\no que é que podes fazer?\n\n38\n00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.097\nEntão, se algum produto estiver em falta,\npor exemplo as bananas,\n\n39\n00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.877\ndevo avisar logo o responsável da secção\npara que seja mais facilmente resolvido.\n\n40\n00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.777\nOk, pronto. Boa. Então,\nquais são as prioridades agora?\n\n41\n00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.437\nEu vou buscar as bananas\ne repor rapidamente.\n\n42\n00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.857\nE eu vou manter a secção organizada e\nretirar aquilo que não está em condições.\n\n43\n00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.157\nOk, parece-me um ótimo plano.\n\n44\n00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.700\nObrigada pelo vosso esforço.\n\n45\n00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.060\nE vocês sabem que nesta secção\na comunicação é chave mesmo, ou seja,\n\n46\n00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.437\nvocês sabem que os clientes são\nmuito exigentes com os nossos legumes,\n\n47\n00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.157\ncom a fruta. E eu sei que tudo\ntem que estar apresentável,\n\n48\n00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.537\na qualidade tem que estar a ser garantida.\n\n49\n00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.977\nObrigada também pelo vosso\nesforço e pela vossa dedicação,\n\n50\n00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.557\ne eu sei que posso contar\ncom vocês neste esforço conjunto.\n\n51\n00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.097\nVamos pensar agora num plano\naqui para a frente para,\n\n52\n00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.917\nse voltarmos a repetir esta situação,\npodermos mudar?\n\n53\n00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.217\nPara já, vamos ver como\né que corre o que combinámos aqui hoje.\n\n54\n00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.437\nDaqui a dois dias voltamos a falar e vemos\nse é preciso rever algum aspeto, ok?\n\n55\n00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.110\n– Ok.\n– Obrigada.\n", "4h": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.147\n– Rita, tens um minuto?\n– Sim, diz.\n\n2\n00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:18.287\nTenho reparado que tens saído\nfora de horas, mais tarde,\n\n3\n00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.010\ne pareces mais silenciosa.\n\n4\n00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.638\n– Está tudo bem?\n– Sim, é só trabalho acumulado.\n\n5\n00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.647\nOk, se precisares de falar, estou aqui.\n\n6\n00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.807\nNão precisas de dizer\nagora se não quiseres,\n\n7\n00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.008\nmas quero que saibas que estou aqui.\n\n8\n00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.947\nSinceramente, não estou\na conseguir dar resposta a tudo.\n\n9\n00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:42.260\nSinto que estou sempre em atraso e começo\na ficar ansiosa só de olhar para o email.\n\n10\n00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.387\nObrigada por teres partilhado isso comigo.\n\n11\n00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.207\nParece mesmo que estás sobrecarregada.\n\n12\n00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.947\nA verdade é que, com o volume\nde trabalho que temos tido,\n\n13\n00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.287\né natural sentires-te assim.\n\n14\n00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:57.507\nSão sintomas comuns e naturais\nquando há uma sobrecarga significativa.\n\n15\n00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.410\nO que é que te tem sido\nassim mais difícil de lidar?\n\n16\n00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.967\nSão os prazos,\né o trabalho ou outra coisa?\n\n17\n00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.887\nÉ tudo junto,\nmas… mas sobretudo os prazos.\n\n18\n00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.794\nSinto que não consigo desligar.\n\n19\n00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.707\nOk, vamos simplificar isto juntas.\n\n20\n00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:17.667\nQuais são aquelas duas ou três coisas que\ntu consideras mais urgentes neste momento?\n\n21\n00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.907\nEntão, a campanha deste mês, o relatório\nsemanal e a apresentação desta semana.\n\n22\n00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.867\nOk, então vais focar-te só na campanha.\n\n23\n00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.707\nEu vou redistribuir o relatório semanal.\n\n24\n00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:34.827\nE, relativamente à apresentação, ajustamos\no prazo se vires que é necessário, ok?\n\n25\n00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.087\nOk, obrigada.\n\n26\n00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.307\nE hoje não vais trabalhar até tarde,\n\n27\n00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.037\nporque o descanso é importante\ne tu precisas de descansar, ok?\n\n28\n00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:43.490\nOk, obrigada.\n\n29\n00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.927\nSe sentires que isto continua, podemos\nver outras formas de te ajudar melhor.\n\n30\n00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.186\nNão tens que passar por isto sozinha.\n\n31\n00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.987\nE nos próximos dias vamos\nacompanhando isto, ok?\n\n32\n00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.658\nCombinado.\nObrigada por teres falado comigo.\n", "4i": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.317\nOlá, está tudo bem?\n\n2\n00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.637\nNão sei.\n\n3\n00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.617\nO meu coração está a disparar,\nestá a bater muito rápido.\n\n4\n00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:30.837\nNão consigo respirar.\n\n5\n00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.920\nCalma, calma, está tudo bem.\n\n6\n00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.617\nEu estou aqui consigo, está bem?\n\n7\n00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.465\nPodemos ir para um sítio\nmais tranquilo, só as duas?\n\n8\n00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.567\nAi, acho que vou desmaiar.\n\n9\n00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.487\nCalma, está tudo bem,\nestá tudo bem, eu estou aqui consigo.\n\n10\n00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.287\nEu sei que está a parecer algo\nmuito desconfortável agora,\n\n11\n00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.147\nmas aquilo que me está a parecer\né que isto é um ataque de pânico.\n\n12\n00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.327\nOu seja, por muito desconfortável que\nesteja a ser e esteja a ser muito intenso,\n\n13\n00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:05.567\nnão é algo perigoso, está bem?\n\n14\n00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.567\nVamos experimentar\nrespirar um pouquinho agora.\n\n15\n00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.150\nVou pedir para inspirar pelo nariz\ne expirar pela boca, assim, profundamente.\n\n16\n00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.210\nConsegue?\n\n17\n00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.727\nEstá a conseguir sentir-se melhor?\n\n18\n00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.467\nEstá a melhorar um bocadinho.\n\n19\n00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.580\nOk, vamos agora sentar.\n\n20\n00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.630\nIsso, procure inspirar.\n\n21\n00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.197\nInspirar, isso.\n\n22\n00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.677\nVamos experimentar aqui\noutro exercício agora?\n\n23\n00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.337\n– Ok, pode ser?\n– Pode.\n\n24\n00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.077\nPronto, olhe, eu vou pedir para ver\n\n25\n00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.390\nse consegue dizer-me cinco\ncoisas que consegue ver.\n\n26\n00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000\nCinco?\n\n27\n00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.620\nA cadeira, a mesa, a parede,\n\n28\n00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.877\na si e a porta.\n\n29\n00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.697\nBoa, muito bem. E agora consegue\ndizer-me quatro coisas que consegue tocar?\n\n30\n00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.917\nAs minhas calças,\n\n31\n00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.830\na parede, o meu casaco\n\n32\n00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.267\ne a camisola.\n\n33\n00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.347\nMuito bem, boa. Boa, e agora,\n\n34\n00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.907\nconsegue-me dizer três\ncoisas que consegue cheirar?\n\n35\n00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:15.990\nCheirar?\n\n36\n00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.740\nO seu perfume,\n\n37\n00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.807\nesta sala,\n\n38\n00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.427\no cheiro da sala\n\n39\n00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:33.427\ne o cheiro ao meu perfume.\n\n40\n00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.330\nBoa, e agora, consegue dizer-me\nduas coisas que consegue ouvir?\n\n41\n00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.327\nConsegue ouvir algum som?\n\n42\n00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:45.410\nA sua voz e o barulho de pessoas lá fora.\n\n43\n00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.760\nIsso, boa. Consegue provar alguma coisa?\n\n44\n00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.177\nEu acho que tenho aqui um rebuçado.\n\n45\n00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.260\nQue bom.\n\n46\n00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.580\nMuito bem, está aqui, está segura.\n\n47\n00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.157\nIsso é o mais importante.\n\n48\n00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.917\nQuer que lhe traga algum\ncopo de água, alguma coisa?\n\n49\n00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.057\nNão, já estou melhor.\n\n50\n00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:02.537\nObrigada.\n\n51\n00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:09.437\nPronto. Precisa que ligue a alguém,\nalguém que a possa vir cá buscar,\n\n52\n00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.820\npara não estar agora sozinha?\n\n53\n00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.730\nPode ficar aqui o tempo\nque necessitar também.\n\n54\n00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.957\nSim, pode-me ajudar a mandar uma mensagem\nao meu marido para ele me vir buscar?\n\n55\n00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.417\nClaro que sim, nós mandamos\numa mensagem juntas, está bem?\n\n56\n00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.917\nIsto que lhe aconteceu é algo normal,\n\n57\n00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.280\npode acontecer quando nós estamos\nem alturas de maior stress,\n\n58\n00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.637\nmas está tudo bem,\nestá bem aqui connosco, está bem?\n\n59\n00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.897\nVamos agora mandar uma mensagem\n\n60\n00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.677\npara alguém a vir buscar\ne depois também aconselhava\n\n61\n00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.257\nquando estamos em momentos de maior\nstress, se calhar, às vezes,\n\n62\n00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:40.399\nfaz bem falarmos com alguém,\nprocurarmos algum profissional de saúde.\n\n63\n00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.113\nObrigada por não me ter deixado sozinha.\n"}, "en": {"01": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.737\nLet's talk about the prevalence\nof mental health problems in Portugal.\n\n2\n00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.327\nPortugal is one of the European countries\n\n3\n00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.597\nwith the highest prevalence\nof mental health problems.\n\n4\n00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.177\nRecent studies indicate\nthat over a third of the adult\n\n5\n00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.617\npopulation shows symptoms\nof anxiety or depression,\n\n6\n00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.037\nand many of these cases do not receive\n\n7\n00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.890\nadequate clinical\nor psychological support.\n\n8\n00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.708\nThese numbers are not just statistics.\n\n9\n00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.702\nBehind every percentage,\nthere are people in real distress.\n\n10\n00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.367\nThis distress often remains invisible,\nnot because it doesn't exist,\n\n11\n00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.857\nbut because it goes unrecognised,\nby the person or by those around them.\n\n12\n00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.837\nIn this context, work plays\na particularly relevant role.\n\n13\n00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.037\nIt can act as a protective\nfactor by offering structure,\n\n14\n00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.149\npurpose and a sense of belonging.\n\n15\n00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.133\nHowever, it can also be a source\nof pressure, burnout and exclusion.\n\n16\n00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.247\nThe way we organise\nand experience work therefore has\n\n17\n00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.833\na very decisive influence\non our mental health.\n", "12": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.707\nAccording to the World\nHealth Organization,\n\n2\n00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.997\nmental health is a state of well-being\n\n3\n00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.827\nin which a person recognises\ntheir own abilities,\n\n4\n00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.787\ncan cope with the challenges of life,\n\n5\n00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.022\nwork productively and contribute\nto their community.\n\n6\n00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.557\nWell-being can be described\nas the perception of emotional balance,\n\n7\n00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.667\nlife satisfaction and a sense of purpose.\n\n8\n00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:27.217\nAt work, it means the experience\nof feeling valued,\n\n9\n00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.865\nsafe, competent and in balance\nwith the different areas of life.\n\n10\n00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.218\nIt should be stressed that mental health\nand well-being are not static states.\n\n11\n00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.507\nThey vary over time\nand depending on the context.\n\n12\n00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.037\nWork, as a central part of daily life,\n\n13\n00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.927\nhas a direct impact on this variation.\n\n14\n00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.707\nThinking about mental health\ntherefore necessarily means\n\n15\n00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.957\nconsidering the context the person is in,\n\n16\n00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.697\nincluding working conditions,\ninterpersonal relationships,\n\n17\n00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.127\nthe organisation of tasks\nand the organisational culture.\n\n18\n00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.427\nRecognising this interdependence\nis an essential step\n\n19\n00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.113\ntowards creating healthier\nand more sustainable environments.\n", "13": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.277\nLet's talk about the role of organisations\nin promoting mental health.\n\n2\n00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.947\nOrganisations play a strategic role\n\n3\n00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.817\nin promoting their employees'\nmental health.\n\n4\n00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.827\nNot only because work takes up\na significant part of people's lives,\n\n5\n00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:18.057\nbut also because it is in this context\nthat many of their relationships,\n\n6\n00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.677\nroutines and demands take shape.\n\n7\n00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.267\nIt is often in the workplace\n\n8\n00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:26.607\nthat the first signs\nof psychological distress appear,\n\n9\n00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.657\nsuch as changes in behaviour,\nextreme tiredness, irritability,\n\n10\n00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.617\ndifficulty concentrating or isolation.\n\n11\n00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.527\nWhen organisations\nare attentive and prepared,\n\n12\n00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.477\nthese signs can be recognised\nin good time,\n\n13\n00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.589\nallowing a more appropriate response.\n\n14\n00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.997\nBeyond identifying distress,\n\n15\n00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.797\nthe greatest potential\nof organisations lies in prevention.\n\n16\n00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.707\nPromoting healthy environments\nmeans developing an organisational\n\n17\n00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.837\nculture centred on respect, listening,\n\n18\n00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.208\nfairness and valuing people.\n\n19\n00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.737\nWhen these conditions are present,\nrelationships become more cooperative,\n\n20\n00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:04.797\nmotivation increases,\n\n21\n00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.538\nproductivity improves and the risk\nof burnout and absenteeism falls.\n\n22\n00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.107\nAt the same time,\nfactors such as work-life balance,\n\n23\n00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:19.057\njob security, trust in leadership\nand access to psychological support\n\n24\n00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.112\nare key elements in promoting well-being.\n\n25\n00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.577\nPromoting mental health at work\nis not an optional extra,\n\n26\n00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.417\nbut an essential dimension\nof how organisations function.\n\n27\n00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.467\nAll organisations,\nwhatever their size or sector,\n\n28\n00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.325\nhave an active role in this process.\n", "21": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.807\nPsychosocial risks\nare characteristics of work,\n\n2\n00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.637\nsuch as the way it is organised,\n\n3\n00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.377\nthe content of tasks\nor the quality of relationships,\n\n4\n00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.184\nthat can harm people's physical,\nemotional and social health.\n\n5\n00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.517\nAlthough they are not directly visible,\n\n6\n00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.297\nlike broken equipment or an unsafe space,\n\n7\n00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.737\ntheir effects are real and often profound.\n\n8\n00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.457\nProlonged exposure to these risks\n\n9\n00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.727\nis associated with higher\nlevels of stress, burnout,\n\n10\n00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.707\nanxiety, depression\nand also physical symptoms,\n\n11\n00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.183\nsuch as muscle pain or sleep problems.\n\n12\n00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.187\nAmong the most common\nexamples are work overload,\n\n13\n00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.567\nconstant pressure for results,\nlack of recognition,\n\n14\n00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.980\ninsecurity about the future,\nor conflicts with colleagues and managers.\n\n15\n00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.047\nWhen these situations persist\nover time, they can contribute\n\n16\n00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.024\nto toxic and harmful working environments.\n\n17\n00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.207\nIt should be stressed that these risks\nare not limited to specific sectors;\n\n18\n00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.937\nthey can exist in any area,\nrole or organisation.\n\n19\n00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.677\nFor this reason, they must be recognised,\nassessed and managed systematically,\n\n20\n00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.638\njust like any other occupational risk.\n\n21\n00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.417\nCreating psychologically\nsafe working environments\n\n22\n00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.457\nis not just an ethical obligation,\n\n23\n00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.607\nit is also an effective strategy\nfor promoting well-being\n\n24\n00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.708\nand improving organisational performance.\n\n25\n00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.297\nSo let's talk about the types\nof psychosocial risk at work.\n\n26\n00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.400\nFirst, job demands.\n\n27\n00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.157\nJob demands refer to the volume\n\n28\n00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.702\nand intensity of the tasks we are given.\n\n29\n00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.847\nThis dimension includes\nquantitative demands,\n\n30\n00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.717\nwhich concern the amount of work,\n\n31\n00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.674\ntight deadlines and excessive workload.\n\n32\n00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.327\nA high and constant pace of work\n\n33\n00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.606\ncan create a sense of permanent urgency.\n\n34\n00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.457\nCognitive demands involve staying\nfocused, making quick decisions,\n\n35\n00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.447\nhandling complex information,\nand emotional demands involve,\n\n36\n00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.377\nfor example,\ndealing with other people's suffering,\n\n37\n00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.737\nhiding emotions or managing\nfrequent conflicts.\n\n38\n00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.207\nWhen these demands are constant\nand not matched by resources or support,\n\n39\n00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.537\nthey can lead to physical\nand emotional exhaustion.\n\n40\n00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.810\nThe second type of risk is work\norganisation and content.\n\n41\n00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.077\nHere we look at how work is structured\nand at the content of tasks.\n\n42\n00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.007\nThis dimension includes influence at work,\n\n43\n00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.577\nwhich concerns the degree of autonomy:\n\n44\n00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.837\ncan we make decisions\nor only carry out orders?\n\n45\n00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.247\nThe possibility of development,\n\n46\n00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.627\nwhich refers to opportunities for learning\n\n47\n00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.517\nand growth. The meaning of work,\n\n48\n00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.537\nwhich relates to the perception\nthat what we do has value.\n\n49\n00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.127\nCommitment to the workplace,\nreflected in involvement,\n\n50\n00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:54.037\nidentification with the organisation's\ngoals and a sense of belonging.\n\n51\n00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.137\nAnd environments that do not allow growth\n\n52\n00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.387\nor recognise the value of the work done\n\n53\n00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:02.217\ntend to create frustration\nand demotivation.\n\n54\n00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.037\nThe third type of risk is social\nrelations and leadership.\n\n55\n00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.197\nThis is one of the most important\nareas for mental health at work.\n\n56\n00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.937\nThis dimension includes predictability,\n\n57\n00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.275\nwhich relates to clarity\nof roles and expectations.\n\n58\n00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.567\nReward can be financial,\n\n59\n00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.057\nbut it also includes\nrecognition and appreciation.\n\n60\n00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.747\nTransparency is therefore\nessential for trust:\n\n61\n00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.537\nknowing what is expected of us,\nhow we are assessed,\n\n62\n00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.637\nwhat decisions are being made.\n\n63\n00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.747\nRole conflict arises when there\nare contradictory demands\n\n64\n00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.837\nor a lack of clearly\ndefined responsibilities.\n\n65\n00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.707\nThe quality of leadership is decisive.\n\n66\n00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.017\nAuthoritarian, absent or inconsistent\nleadership creates tension and insecurity.\n\n67\n00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.357\nSocial support from colleagues\nand managers acts as a protective factor.\n\n68\n00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.807\nWhen there is empathy,\navailability and collaboration,\n\n69\n00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.017\nthe impact of stress tends to be smaller.\n\n70\n00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.957\nPoor or toxic relationships\nat work are one of the main\n\n71\n00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.497\ncauses of emotional distress\nand of people leaving their jobs.\n\n72\n00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.513\nThe fourth type of risk\nis the work-individual interface.\n\n73\n00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.037\nThis type of risk relates to the way\n\n74\n00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.430\nwork fits in with other areas of life.\n\n75\n00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.807\nThis dimension includes job insecurity,\n\n76\n00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.267\nsuch as fear of losing one's\njob or unstable contracts,\n\n77\n00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.217\nwhich causes prolonged anxiety.\nJob satisfaction,\n\n78\n00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.937\nwhich is an important indicator\nof well-being: if we are dissatisfied,\n\n79\n00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.137\nsymptoms of burnout or demotivation\nare more likely to appear.\n\n80\n00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.097\nWork-family conflict arises\n\n81\n00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.027\nwhen professional demands\ninterfere with personal life,\n\n82\n00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.717\nleaving too little time\nto be with the children,\n\n83\n00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.189\nrest or look after one's health.\n\n84\n00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.337\nThese are some of the most\nconcrete examples.\n\n85\n00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.927\nEnvironments that do not respect\nthis balance undermine\n\n86\n00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.077\nworkers' health and their productivity\nin the medium term.\n\n87\n00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.257\nThe fifth type of psychosocial\nrisk is values in the workplace.\n\n88\n00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.637\nOrganisational culture strongly influences\n\n89\n00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.117\nhow people feel in their day-to-day lives.\n\n90\n00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.557\nThis dimension includes\nvertical trust, that is,\n\n91\n00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.597\nbetween those who lead\nand those who are led,\n\n92\n00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.997\nand this trust is essential\nfor good communication\n\n93\n00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.178\nand for resolving conflicts and problems.\n\n94\n00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.967\nHorizontal trust, that is,\nbetween colleagues,\n\n95\n00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.257\nmakes collaboration\nand team spirit easier.\n\n96\n00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.027\nJustice and respect\ninvolve fair treatment,\n\n97\n00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.177\nrecognition of differences\nand active listening.\n\n98\n00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.077\nSocial community at work refers\nto the quality of informal relationships,\n\n99\n00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.557\nthe sense of belonging,\nthe idea that we are part of something.\n\n100\n00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.467\nWhen values are out of step with practice\n\n101\n00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.497\nor when there is institutional hypocrisy,\n\n102\n00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:47.794\nthe effects are corrosive to employees'\nmotivation and mental health.\n\n103\n00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.857\nThe sixth type of risk\nis offensive behaviour.\n\n104\n00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.377\nThis is one of the most serious risks\nand one of the hardest to report.\n\n105\n00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.117\nThis dimension includes bullying,\nwith repeated humiliation,\n\n106\n00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.628\nbelittling, sabotage or social isolation.\n\n107\n00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.467\nSexual harassment, with unwanted comments,\n\n108\n00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.057\ngestures or behaviour of a sexual nature.\n\n109\n00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:16.637\nDiscrimination based on gender,\nethnicity, sexual orientation, age,\n\n110\n00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.593\ndisability, among others.\n\n111\n00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:24.505\nVerbal or physical violence,\neven if subtle or disguised.\n\n112\n00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.937\nThese behaviours we have talked\nabout violate basic human rights\n\n113\n00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.237\nand have a devastating impact\non mental health and self-esteem.\n\n114\n00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.807\nPsychosocial risks are not inevitable,\n\n115\n00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.417\nbut they arise when work\nis poorly planned or managed,\n\n116\n00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:42.606\nor when the social context\ndoes not protect the worker.\n\n117\n00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.487\nIdentifying them is the first step\nto preventing psychological distress\n\n118\n00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.060\nand promoting healthier workplaces.\n", "24": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.597\nLet's talk about the individual factors\nthat influence how risks are perceived.\n\n2\n00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.017\nEach person is unique, and this shows\nin the way they experience work.\n\n3\n00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.267\nTwo people can be in the same team,\nwith the same tasks,\n\n4\n00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.507\nthe same hours and the same managers\n\n5\n00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.697\nand still have completely\ndifferent experiences.\n\n6\n00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.617\nWhile one feels motivated and calm,\n\n7\n00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.997\nthe other may feel exhausted,\nanxious or on the verge of giving up.\n\n8\n00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.237\nWhat explains this difference?\n\n9\n00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.867\nThe answer lies in individual\nfactors, that is,\n\n10\n00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:34.237\neverything each person brings\nwith them into the workplace.\n\n11\n00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.627\nThese factors influence how\nwe interpret situations,\n\n12\n00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.536\nreact to stress and use,\nor don't use, regulation strategies.\n\n13\n00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.644\nHere are some of the main ones.\n\n14\n00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.857\nAdverse life experiences.\n\n15\n00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.397\nPeople who have been through\ndifficult situations in their lives,\n\n16\n00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.337\nsuch as neglect, violence,\n\n17\n00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.397\ngrief or poverty,\nmay be more sensitive to stress.\n\n18\n00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.207\nCertain situations at work\ncan trigger emotional\n\n19\n00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.017\nresponses linked to past experiences,\n\n20\n00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.037\neven if the current\nrisk is not equivalent.\n\n21\n00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.057\nFor example, a person\n\n22\n00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.747\nwho experienced housing\ninsecurity as a child may react\n\n23\n00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.207\nwith high anxiety to a company\nrestructuring announcement,\n\n24\n00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.057\neven before knowing whether\nthey will be directly affected.\n\n25\n00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.657\nFamily history and genetic factors.\n\n26\n00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.467\nMental disorders in the family,\nsuch as depression or anxiety,\n\n27\n00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.227\ncan increase individual vulnerability,\n\n28\n00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.307\nespecially when there\nare no developed coping\n\n29\n00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.755\nstrategies or adequate support.\n\n30\n00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.757\nFor example, an employee whose\nmother had severe depression\n\n31\n00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.627\nmay take criticism\nat work more personally,\n\n32\n00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.393\nexperiencing it as rejection.\n\n33\n00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.967\nBiological factors. Biological\naspects such as hormonal changes,\n\n34\n00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.077\nsleep disorders, chronic illness\nor persistent pain directly affect\n\n35\n00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.877\nthe capacity for emotional\nregulation and tolerance of stress.\n\n36\n00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.077\nOur response to work is not only\npsychological, it is also physical.\n\n37\n00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.837\nTemperament and psychological\ncharacteristics.\n\n38\n00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.017\nIndividual differences in temperament,\n\n39\n00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.417\nsuch as a greater tendency towards\nanxiety, impulsiveness or perfectionism,\n\n40\n00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.997\nshape how each person responds to demands,\n\n41\n00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.517\ndeadlines or situations of uncertainty.\n\n42\n00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.977\nFor example, a person\n\n43\n00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.207\nwith perfectionist traits\nmay feel great discomfort\n\n44\n00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.858\nin ambiguous settings or where mistakes\nare more frequent and inevitable.\n\n45\n00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.677\nSocioeconomic conditions.\n\n46\n00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.237\nFinancial instability,\n\n47\n00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.697\nprecarious housing\nor responsibility for dependants\n\n48\n00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.097\nincrease the pressure on the individual\nand reduce their room to adapt.\n\n49\n00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.837\nThese conditions tend to amplify\nthe impact of psychosocial risks.\n\n50\n00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:49.827\nFor example, a worker\n\n51\n00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:53.647\nwho depends solely\non their salary to support\n\n52\n00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.543\ntheir family may tolerate adverse\nconditions for fear of losing their job.\n\n53\n00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.389\nSubstance use.\n\n54\n00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.777\nThe use of alcohol,\n\n55\n00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.947\nmedication or other substances\ncan be a risk factor,\n\n56\n00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.768\nbut also an attempt to cope\nwith stress or overload.\n\n57\n00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.577\nHowever, when used as the main strategy,\n\n58\n00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.337\nit tends to make the problem worse,\ncreating cycles of greater vulnerability.\n\n59\n00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.897\nFamily and social support.\n\n60\n00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.077\nA strong support network,\nwhether family, social or professional,\n\n61\n00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.481\nacts as an important protective factor.\n\n62\n00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.407\nPeople who feel supported tend\n\n63\n00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.137\nto recover more quickly\nfrom difficult situations.\n\n64\n00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.007\nFor example, after a conflict at work,\n\n65\n00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.387\nbeing able to share the experience\n\n66\n00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.017\nwith someone you trust can significantly\nreduce the emotional impact.\n\n67\n00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.027\nPhysical health.\nHealth problems such as chronic fatigue,\n\n68\n00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.477\npersistent pain or long-term illness\n\n69\n00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.647\ncan undermine the ability\nto cope with work demands,\n\n70\n00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.699\nboth physical and emotional.\n\n71\n00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.085\nPsychosocial risks\ndo not act in isolation.\n\n72\n00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.367\nTheir impact is deeply influenced\n\n73\n00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.664\nby each person's\nindividual characteristics.\n\n74\n00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.117\nPromoting healthy working\nenvironments therefore means not\n\n75\n00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.707\nonly reducing external risk factors,\n\n76\n00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.247\nbut also recognising\nthe diversity of experiences,\n\n77\n00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.838\npaths and individual needs within teams.\n", "36": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.537\nLet's talk about risk factors\nand protective factors.\n\n2\n00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.807\nWhen someone goes through\na situation of intense stress\n\n3\n00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:12.497\nor potential trauma, how they react\ndoes not depend only on the event itself.\n\n4\n00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.877\nIt also depends on a set\nof factors that exist before,\n\n5\n00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.357\nduring and after the situation.\n\n6\n00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.477\nAnd these can increase the risk\nof lasting distress or, on the contrary,\n\n7\n00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.501\nsupport a healthy recovery.\n\n8\n00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.917\nBefore the crisis:\nprior vulnerability and protection.\n\n9\n00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.757\nEven before the event happens,\n\n10\n00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.017\nsome people already\nhave vulnerability factors.\n\n11\n00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.027\nThese include a history of trauma\n\n12\n00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.717\nor adverse experiences throughout life,\n\n13\n00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.490\nas well as previously\nfragile mental health.\n\n14\n00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.327\nThese elements can increase\nsensitivity to stress\n\n15\n00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.277\nand make emotional regulation\nharder in demanding situations.\n\n16\n00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:52.387\nOn the other hand,\n\n17\n00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:57.317\nthere are also prior protective factors\nthat can soften the impact of a crisis.\n\n18\n00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.727\nThese include positive social bonds,\n\n19\n00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:06.027\nwith family, colleagues or the community\nat large, as well as emotional literacy\n\n20\n00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.952\nand the use of effective\ncoping strategies.\n\n21\n00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.997\nHaving overcome hardship before\n\n22\n00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.277\nand organisational settings\nmarked by a culture of care,\n\n23\n00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.517\nprevention and listening\nare also protective elements.\n\n24\n00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.297\nThese factors do not eliminate\nthe impact of the crisis,\n\n25\n00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.277\nbut they significantly\ninfluence how it is experienced.\n\n26\n00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.917\nDuring the crisis:\nthe immediate response of the context.\n\n27\n00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.377\nDuring a critical moment,\nsuch as an accident,\n\n28\n00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.367\nan intense conflict, a dismissal\nor another destabilising event,\n\n29\n00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.658\nfactors in the immediate context\ncan worsen or ease the distress.\n\n30\n00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.187\nA lack of clear\nand empathetic communication,\n\n31\n00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.677\ndisorganised or absent leadership,\n\n32\n00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.767\nthe emotional isolation\nof the person affected, or punitive\n\n33\n00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.654\nand dismissive responses\nfrom the organisation\n\n34\n00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.625\nare important risk factors.\n\n35\n00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.957\nIn contrast, immediate support can act\n\n36\n00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.099\nas a significant protective factor.\n\n37\n00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.097\nBeing with someone\nwho listens with empathy,\n\n38\n00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.407\nacknowledgement of the impact\nof the situation,\n\n39\n00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.967\na physically and emotionally\nsafe space to react\n\n40\n00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.817\nand rapid intervention by someone trained\n\n41\n00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.417\nin psychological first aid are elements\n\n42\n00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.417\nthat contribute to initial stabilisation.\n\n43\n00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:23.947\nIn the workplace,\n\n44\n00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.977\nthe way the organisation responds\nat this moment can be decisive.\n\n45\n00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.357\nAfter the crisis: recovery and follow-up.\n\n46\n00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.767\nThe weeks and months after\nthe situation are equally important\n\n47\n00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.817\nfor the recovery process.\n\n48\n00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.437\nA quick return to activities\nwithout adequate recovery time,\n\n49\n00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.127\ninstitutional silence or denial\nof the impact of the situation,\n\n50\n00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.097\na lack of psychological support\n\n51\n00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.157\nand ongoing adverse working conditions,\nsuch as excessive pressure,\n\n52\n00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.257\nconflict or isolation,\ncan worsen the distress.\n\n53\n00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.567\nOn the other hand,\nfollow-up by managers or peers,\n\n54\n00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.287\naccess to psychological support,\nacts of care and solidarity and,\n\n55\n00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.720\nwhere necessary,\nadapting working conditions\n\n56\n00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.277\nact as important protective\nfactors at this stage.\n\n57\n00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.777\nIn short, the impact of a crisis\ndoes not depend only on the event itself,\n\n58\n00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.207\nbut on all the individual and\norganisational conditions that precede it,\n\n59\n00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:23.597\naccompany it and follow it.\n\n60\n00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.877\nActing on these factors allows\n\n61\n00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.787\na more structured\napproach to mental health,\n\n62\n00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.657\nfocused not only on responding to crises,\n\n63\n00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.473\nbut also on preventing\nand managing them over time.\n", "4a": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.085\nOne of the self-regulation strategies\nyou can use is controlled breathing.\n\n2\n00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.857\nSo I'm going to teach you a few tricks\n\n3\n00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.940\nso that you can do it effectively.\n\n4\n00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.097\nFirst, I'd like you to sit\nsomewhere comfortable,\n\n5\n00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.718\nwith your feet firmly on the floor.\n\n6\n00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.197\nThen, the aim is to breathe in through\nyour nose and out through your mouth.\n\n7\n00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.029\nIf it helps, you can think of smelling\na flower, blowing out a candle.\n\n8\n00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.417\nNext, I'd like you to place one\nof your hands on top of your chest\n\n9\n00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.587\nand the other hand below your chest,\n\n10\n00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.650\nso that you can feel\nyour breathing better.\n\n11\n00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.098\nAnd now the aim is to breathe in,\nhold and breathe out, ok?\n\n12\n00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.555\nI'll do it with you to make it easier.\n\n13\n00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.304\nBreathe in, hold,\n\n14\n00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.707\nbreathe out.\n\n15\n00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.351\nLet's do this counting in seconds.\n\n16\n00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.447\nFive seconds to breathe in,\nthree seconds to hold,\n\n17\n00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.573\nfive seconds to breathe out.\n\n18\n00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.178\nDo it with me.\n\n19\n00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.467\nWith this breathing,\nyou should feel your chest, that is,\n\n20\n00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:33.639\nboth your hands rising at the same time.\n\n21\n00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:39.915\nThe air above and below your chest\nhas to come in at the same time.\n\n22\n00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.980\nLet's do it again.\n\n23\n00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.819\nBreathe in for five,\nhold for three and breathe out for five.\n\n24\n00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.177\nThe aim of this breathing\nis to renew the air in your lungs,\n\n25\n00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.777\nslow down your heart\nand focus you on a simple task\n\n26\n00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.566\nthat will make you think and bring\nyou into the present moment.\n", "4b": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.797\nGrounding is a self-regulation\nstrategy that helps\n\n2\n00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.337\nbring us into the present moment\nand often helps us break out\n\n3\n00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.971\nof a vicious cycle of thoughts.\n\n4\n00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.507\nThe truth is that we can't\nsilence the mind,\n\n5\n00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.894\nbut we can direct it.\n\n6\n00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:27.357\nSo giving our brain a simple\ntask helps us self-regulate.\n\n7\n00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:32.667\nTo do this, we'll use our five senses,\nin descending order.\n\n8\n00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.997\nThe aim is for you to tell me,\nin your surroundings,\n\n9\n00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.400\nfive things you can see.\n\n10\n00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.351\nFour things you can touch.\n\n11\n00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.177\nWhen you notice these\nthings you can touch,\n\n12\n00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.832\nI suggest you touch them and try\nto feel their different textures.\n\n13\n00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.824\nSo, four things you can touch.\n\n14\n00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.849\nThree things you can hear.\n\n15\n00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.065\nTwo things you can smell.\n\n16\n00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.220\nAnd finally, one thing you can taste.\n\n17\n00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.917\nAt this point, if you actually\nhave something you can taste,\n\n18\n00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.377\nit's important to savour it slowly,\nwhether it's a sweet, a piece of fruit,\n\n19\n00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.984\na chocolate, whatever it may be.\n\n20\n00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.317\nAs for the things we sometimes think about\n\n21\n00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.277\nor ask ourselves and can't find\nthe answer to, for example,\n\n22\n00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.704\ntwo things I can smell.\n\n23\n00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.827\nSometimes we can't smell two things\n\n24\n00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.527\nor we can't pick up two different smells.\n\n25\n00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.237\nWhat I invite you to do then is:\neither ignore it,\n\n26\n00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.507\nif you feel you're still very activated,\nor draw on your memory,\n\n27\n00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.237\nbecause our brain has a capacity\nfor memory and I can think, ok,\n\n28\n00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.781\nI can't smell two things,\n\n29\n00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.802\nbut I can remember a smell I really like.\n\n30\n00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.227\nFor example,\nI really like the smell of the sea\n\n31\n00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.177\nand my brain will automatically\n\n32\n00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.189\nrecall that feeling and help me allow\n\n33\n00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:11.213\nthat feeling to flow through my body\nand help me self-regulate, ok?\n\n34\n00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.597\nSo, to recap, five things you can see,\nfour things you can touch,\n\n35\n00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.574\nthree things you can hear,\n\n36\n00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.814\ntwo things you can smell\nand one thing you can taste.\n", "4c": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.827\nThe STOP strategy is one\nthat can be used anywhere,\n\n2\n00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:14.728\nbecause it's a strategy\nyou can do quietly or even in your head.\n\n3\n00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.205\nSTOP, as in \"stop\".\n\n4\n00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.583\nStop what you're doing.\n\n5\n00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.826\nTake a breath.\n\n6\n00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.487\nBreathe slowly, once or twice,\nfor one or two minutes,\n\n7\n00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.771\nthe controlled breathing we also\ntalk about in this toolkit.\n\n8\n00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.757\nObserve, that is, observe what's\naround you, observe what's happening,\n\n9\n00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.377\nobserve the sensations in your body,\nyour emotions, your behaviour,\n\n10\n00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.277\nyour thoughts.\n\n11\n00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:46.857\nThen,\n\n12\n00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.553\nchange your perspective.\n\n13\n00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.607\nOk, what's happening?\n\n14\n00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.834\nIs there another way to deal with this?\n\n15\n00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.532\nIs there another explanation for this?\n\n16\n00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.077\nCan I change the way\nI'm thinking about this?\n\n17\n00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.017\nAnd finally, proceed, that is,\n\n18\n00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.217\nstart with small behaviours or actions,\nbut create movement,\n\n19\n00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.940\nthat is, proceed.\n\n20\n00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.507\nThis strategy is extremely important\nand lets us stop and listen to ourselves,\n\n21\n00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.423\nwhich is something extremely\nimportant in self-regulation.\n", "4d": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.327\nSelf-regulation through touch\nis a strategy that can be used\n\n2\n00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.257\nwhen we're under a lot\nof stress or anxiety\n\n3\n00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.412\nand involves seeking regulation\nthrough our own touch.\n\n4\n00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.720\nThere are several, and I'm going\nto teach you four ways of doing it.\n\n5\n00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.057\nThe first one I want to teach you involves\nplacing your hand on your chest,\n\n6\n00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.077\none hand above the chest\nand the other below it.\n\n7\n00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.327\nThis strategy is also used\nin controlled breathing,\n\n8\n00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.570\nwhich is also covered\nhere in this toolkit.\n\n9\n00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.097\nThe aim is for you to feel it,\nbut if you combine it with breathing,\n\n10\n00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.057\nyou also add the sensation\nof feeling your chest\n\n11\n00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.848\nand belly rising and falling.\n\n12\n00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.416\nAnother strategy you can use\ninvolves touching your own hands.\n\n13\n00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.697\nIf we put our hands\nin contact with ourselves,\n\n14\n00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.757\nwe can apply slight pressure,\n\n15\n00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.245\nalmost as if we were comforting\nsomeone. How would we do it?\n\n16\n00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.017\nThat pressure is the pressure\nyou should use on yourself.\n\n17\n00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.787\nAnother strategy you can use\n\n18\n00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.107\nis putting your arms around your body,\n\n19\n00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.747\nalmost as if we were\ngiving ourselves a hug,\n\n20\n00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.961\nalmost as if we were comforting ourselves.\n\n21\n00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.797\nThis self-hug should also have some\npressure, gentle, not suffocating;\n\n22\n00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.509\nit should be calm and comforting, ok?\n\n23\n00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:37.197\nAnd finally, placing your feet\non the floor and pressing down slightly,\n\n24\n00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.857\nso that we notice we are here,\nin the present moment,\n\n25\n00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.865\nalmost like a grounding thought, ok?\n\n26\n00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.647\nThese strategies can be used and done for\n\n27\n00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:51.267\n30 seconds to a minute,\nand the aim is a kind of self-comfort.\n", "4e": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.437\n– Maria, can I talk to you for a bit?\n– Yes, go ahead.\n\n2\n00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.119\nI've noticed you've been quieter,\na bit more withdrawn since the accident.\n\n3\n00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.273\nI wanted to know how you are.\n\n4\n00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.630\nIt hasn't been easy.\n\n5\n00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.557\nDo you want to share how\nyou've felt since the accident?\n\n6\n00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.066\nIt's always on my mind,\nI can't stop thinking about it.\n\n7\n00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.218\nAnd I've already made mistakes\nI don't normally make.\n\n8\n00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.437\nI can imagine,\nit can't have been easy to witness that.\n\n9\n00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.957\nBut the truth is, the reaction\nyou're having is very common\n\n10\n00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.837\nwhen we're present at a moment like that.\n\n11\n00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.744\nThere's nothing wrong with you.\n\n12\n00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.496\nYes, but it's affecting my work.\n\n13\n00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.156\nWhat do you think is hardest for you?\n\n14\n00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.177\nIs it being here, in the same workplace,\nor is it the workload?\n\n15\n00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.417\nI think it's a bit of both.\n\n16\n00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.097\nI can't focus.\n\n17\n00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.617\nIt's not a lack of focus.\n\n18\n00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.657\nIt's a natural reaction\nto what you went through, what you saw.\n\n19\n00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.152\nWhat do you think we can do to make\nthings a bit lighter for you right now?\n\n20\n00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.777\nIf it makes sense to you, MC has several\nways of giving you psychological support.\n\n21\n00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.481\nAnd, if you like,\nI can help you get in touch.\n\n22\n00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.215\nYou don't have to go\nthrough this alone, ok?\n\n23\n00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.919\nThank you, really.\n\n24\n00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.327\nOver the next few days, we'll follow up\n\n25\n00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.347\nto see if the strategies are working and,\n\n26\n00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.113\nif you feel you need something\nbefore then, just tell me, ok?\n\n27\n00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.460\nOk.\n", "4f": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.715\nAre you ok?\n\n2\n00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.705\nYes, it's nothing much.\n\n3\n00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.857\nHmm, you don't seem yourself today.\n\n4\n00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.894\nIf you need to talk, I'm here.\n\n5\n00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.817\nIt's just that this morning I got a call\n\n6\n00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.954\nand my mother has been\ndiagnosed with that illness.\n\n7\n00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.969\nCome here, let's sit down for a bit.\n\n8\n00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.807\nI'm so sorry you're feeling like this,\n\n9\n00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.772\nit must have been very\nhard to receive that news.\n\n10\n00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.664\nYes, I can't stop thinking about\nit and be here working at the same time.\n\n11\n00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.557\nBut it makes sense that you can't, I mean,\n\n12\n00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.507\ncarry on with your day as normal\n\n13\n00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.414\nafter receiving news like that.\n\n14\n00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.021\nYou're processing a lot at the same time.\n\n15\n00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.287\nMy chest feels tight and, at the same\ntime, I have no energy for anything,\n\n16\n00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.047\nI just wanted to leave.\n\n17\n00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.460\nEasy, let's breathe for a moment.\n\n18\n00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.457\nTake a deep breath. What do you think\n\n19\n00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.335\nwould help you most right now?\n\n20\n00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.197\nStaying here a bit longer?\n\n21\n00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.698\nTalking to someone?\n\n22\n00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.131\nGoing home early?\n\n23\n00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.277\nI think today I really need to leave.\n\n24\n00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.477\nLeave early.\n\n25\n00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.266\nThat makes sense. Shall I come\nwith you to talk to our manager?\n\n26\n00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.577\nAnd you don't have to go\nthrough this alone, ok?\n\n27\n00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.977\nThank you. I was really feeling lost.\n\n28\n00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.407\nListen, and if it keeps being very\nhard for you, if it makes sense to you,\n\n29\n00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.177\nyou don't have to go through this alone.\n\n30\n00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.037\nYou can talk to someone.\n\n31\n00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.077\nI know MC has agreements, for example,\n\n32\n00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.117\nyou can talk to mental health\nprofessionals, for example.\n\n33\n00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.548\nBut for now, let's take it easy,\none step at a time, ok?\n\n34\n00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.877\nShall we talk to our manager together?\n\n35\n00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.520\nYes. And thank you for talking to me.\n", "4g": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.087\nI asked you earlier to bring more\nbananas and they're still not here.\n\n2\n00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.287\nThe display is empty.\n\n3\n00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:20.617\nI know, but look, they asked me to remove\nthe fruit that wasn't in good condition\n\n4\n00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.467\nand then to help with the vegetables.\n\n5\n00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.947\nYes, but bananas\nare our best-selling product.\n\n6\n00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:28.547\n– It makes the shop look bad.\n– I know, but I can't be everywhere.\n\n7\n00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.347\nRight, but it seems you never\nfinish anything I ask you to do.\n\n8\n00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.842\nWell, and you act like you're\nthe only one who works here.\n\n9\n00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.638\nAna, Maria, can you come over here?\n\n10\n00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.937\nI know there are a lot\nof customers here today\n\n11\n00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.377\nand that you're both a bit\nstressed about this situation,\n\n12\n00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.519\nbut we need to sort this\nout as quickly as possible.\n\n13\n00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.345\nWhat's going on?\n\n14\n00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.297\nThe bananas are always running out\n\n15\n00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.377\nand I'm here on my own trying\nto keep everything presentable.\n\n16\n00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.217\nI asked for help and got no response.\n\n17\n00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.107\nSo, you're worried about the image\n\n18\n00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.131\nand the display of your products?\n\n19\n00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.767\n– Yes.\n– Maria?\n\n20\n00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.607\nI was removing the fruit that wasn't\npresentable for customers\n\n21\n00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.257\nand then I had to help restock elsewhere.\n\n22\n00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.737\nI couldn't be everywhere.\n\n23\n00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.367\nOk, so you're telling\nme you're a bit worried\n\n24\n00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.391\nabout product upkeep and quality?\n\n25\n00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.197\nYes.\n\n26\n00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.657\nSo, from what I understand,\n\n27\n00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.967\nyou're both trying to meet\nthe section's needs, that is,\n\n28\n00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.393\nit's not a lack of willingness\nto help on either side.\n\n29\n00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.797\nWhat seems to be happening\nis a lack of communication,\n\n30\n00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.971\nand that makes it much harder\nfor us to organise priorities.\n\n31\n00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.368\nSo, Maria, if you get moved\nto another task, what can you do?\n\n32\n00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.667\nI can let Ana or whoever\nis working with me know,\n\n33\n00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.868\nso they know what they can count on.\n\n34\n00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.605\nGreat, that way I can plan ahead.\n\n35\n00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.454\nGood, a very good effort from both of you.\n\n36\n00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.527\nSo, Ana, what about you:\nif a product isn't up to standard,\n\n37\n00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:13.781\nwhat can you do?\n\n38\n00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.107\nWell, if a product runs out,\nthe bananas for example,\n\n39\n00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:22.602\nI should tell the section manager\nstraight away so it's solved more easily.\n\n40\n00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.652\nOk, right. Good. So,\nwhat are the priorities now?\n\n41\n00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.437\nI'll go and get the bananas\nand restock quickly.\n\n42\n00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.857\nAnd I'll keep the section tidy and remove\nanything that isn't in good condition.\n\n43\n00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.016\nOk, that sounds like a great plan.\n\n44\n00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.657\nThank you for your effort.\n\n45\n00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.507\nAnd you know that in this section\ncommunication really is key, that is,\n\n46\n00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.307\nyou know customers are very\ndemanding about our vegetables,\n\n47\n00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.757\nabout the fruit. And I know\neverything has to be presentable,\n\n48\n00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.537\nquality has to be guaranteed.\n\n49\n00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.947\nThank you also for your effort\nand your dedication,\n\n50\n00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.030\nand I know I can count\non you in this joint effort.\n\n51\n00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.327\nLet's now think of a plan\ngoing forward so that,\n\n52\n00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:02.315\nif this situation happens again,\nwe can change things?\n\n53\n00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.217\nFor now, let's see how\nwhat we agreed today works out.\n\n54\n00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.359\nWe'll talk again in two days and see\nif anything needs reviewing, ok?\n\n55\n00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:11.804\n– Ok.\n– Thank you.\n", "4h": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.147\n– Rita, have you got a minute?\n– Yes, go ahead.\n\n2\n00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:18.207\nI've noticed you've been\nleaving late, after hours,\n\n3\n00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.525\nand you seem quieter.\n\n4\n00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.267\n– Is everything ok?\n– Yes, it's just a backlog of work.\n\n5\n00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.645\nOk, if you need to talk, I'm here.\n\n6\n00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.877\nNo need to tell me now\nif you don't want to,\n\n7\n00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.087\nbut I want you to know I'm here.\n\n8\n00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.668\nHonestly, I can't keep up with everything.\n\n9\n00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:43.030\nI feel like I'm always behind and I get\nanxious just looking at my email.\n\n10\n00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.387\nThank you for sharing that with me.\n\n11\n00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.207\nIt really seems like you're overloaded.\n\n12\n00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.437\nThe truth is,\nwith the amount of work we've had,\n\n13\n00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.523\nit's natural to feel like this.\n\n14\n00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.862\nThese are common, natural symptoms\nwhen there's significant overload.\n\n15\n00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.002\nWhat's been hardest for you to deal with?\n\n16\n00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.669\nIs it the deadlines,\nthe work, or something else?\n\n17\n00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.887\nIt's everything together,\nbut… but mostly the deadlines.\n\n18\n00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.337\nI feel like I can't switch off.\n\n19\n00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:12.707\nOk, let's simplify this together.\n\n20\n00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:16.849\nWhat are the two or three things\nyou think are most urgent right now?\n\n21\n00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.907\nWell, this month's campaign, the weekly\nreport and this week's presentation.\n\n22\n00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.000\nOk, so you'll focus only on the campaign.\n\n23\n00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.022\nI'll reassign the weekly report.\n\n24\n00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:33.849\nAnd as for the presentation,\nwe'll adjust the deadline if needed, ok?\n\n25\n00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.062\nOk, thank you.\n\n26\n00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.687\nAnd today you're not working late,\n\n27\n00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.771\nbecause rest is important\nand you need to rest, ok?\n\n28\n00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:43.447\nOk, thank you.\n\n29\n00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.927\nIf you feel this continues, we can look\nat other ways to help you better.\n\n30\n00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.363\nYou don't have to go through this alone.\n\n31\n00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.987\nAnd we'll keep an eye on this\nover the next few days, ok?\n\n32\n00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.777\nDeal. Thank you for talking to me.\n", "4i": "WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.553\nHello, is everything ok?\n\n2\n00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.474\nI don't know.\n\n3\n00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.617\nMy heart is racing,\nit's beating really fast.\n\n4\n00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:30.764\nI can't breathe.\n\n5\n00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.654\nEasy, easy, it's all right.\n\n6\n00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.617\nI'm here with you, ok?\n\n7\n00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.641\nShall we go somewhere quieter,\njust the two of us?\n\n8\n00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.567\nOh, I think I'll faint.\n\n9\n00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.487\nEasy, it's all right,\nit's all right, I'm here with you.\n\n10\n00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.937\nI know this feels very\nuncomfortable right now,\n\n11\n00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.147\nbut what it looks like to me\nis that this is a panic attack.\n\n12\n00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.507\nThat is, however uncomfortable\nand intense it may feel,\n\n13\n00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.567\nit's not dangerous, ok?\n\n14\n00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.567\nLet's try breathing a little now.\n\n15\n00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.127\nBreathe in through your nose and out\nthrough your mouth, deeply, like this.\n\n16\n00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.299\nCan you do it?\n\n17\n00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.552\nAre you starting to feel better?\n\n18\n00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.467\nIt's getting a little better.\n\n19\n00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.213\nOk, let's sit down now.\n\n20\n00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.386\nThat's it, try to breathe in.\n\n21\n00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.197\nBreathe in, that's it.\n\n22\n00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.434\nShall we try another exercise now?\n\n23\n00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.337\n– Ok, is that all right?\n– Yes.\n\n24\n00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.127\nRight, now I'd like to see\n\n25\n00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.008\nif you can tell me five\nthings you can see.\n\n26\n00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000\nFive?\n\n27\n00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.467\nThe chair, the table, the wall,\n\n28\n00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.608\nyou and the door.\n\n29\n00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.960\nGreat, very good. And now can you tell\nme four things you can touch?\n\n30\n00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:58.957\nMy trousers,\n\n31\n00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.040\nthe wall, my coat\n\n32\n00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.250\nand my jumper.\n\n33\n00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.857\nVery good, great. Great, and now,\n\n34\n00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.145\ncan you tell me three\nthings you can smell?\n\n35\n00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:15.990\nSmell?\n\n36\n00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.070\nYour perfume,\n\n37\n00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.507\nthis room,\n\n38\n00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:25.507\nthe smell of the room\n\n39\n00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.260\nand the smell of my perfume.\n\n40\n00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.804\nGreat, and now, can you tell\nme two things you can hear?\n\n41\n00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.327\nCan you hear any sounds?\n\n42\n00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.939\nYour voice and the noise\nof people outside.\n\n43\n00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.102\nThat's it, great. Can you taste anything?\n\n44\n00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.125\nI think I've got a sweet here.\n\n45\n00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.383\nThat's good.\n\n46\n00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.398\nVery good, you're here, you're safe.\n\n47\n00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.157\nThat's what matters most.\n\n48\n00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.917\nShall I get you a glass\nof water, or anything?\n\n49\n00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.057\nNo, I'm better now.\n\n50\n00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:02.537\nThank you.\n\n51\n00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.237\nRight. Do you need me to call someone,\nsomeone who can come and pick you up,\n\n52\n00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.680\nso you're not on your own now?\n\n53\n00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.574\nYou can also stay here\nas long as you need.\n\n54\n00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.957\nYes, could you help me send a message\nto my husband to come and pick me up?\n\n55\n00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.687\nOf course, we'll send\na message together, ok?\n\n56\n00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.877\nWhat happened to you is normal,\n\n57\n00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.707\nit can happen when we're going\nthrough times of greater stress,\n\n58\n00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.637\nbut everything is fine,\nyou're fine here with us, ok?\n\n59\n00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.867\nLet's send a message now\n\n60\n00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.047\nfor someone to come and pick you up,\nand I'd also suggest\n\n61\n00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.727\nthat when we're under more stress,\nperhaps, sometimes,\n\n62\n00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.671\nit helps to talk to someone,\nto seek out a health professional.\n\n63\n00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.054\nThank you for not leaving me on my own.\n"}};
